orts is that  athletes and countries compete with each  other regardless of different ideologies  politics religions and so on  so this should be like really an  exception uh to boycott because  otherwise the international sporting  system would not function i can just  share something from discussions with my  students in my classes so they have been  critical and said they are double  standards because the many western  countries would accept the treatment of  the palestinian people and not boycott  israel why he is now a different action  in the case of russia and ukraine this  is true i share this criticism  but i think wrong acting in one case  doesn't justify to act wrongly in  another case and we are really in a very  critical situation given sense since  russia has um  nuclear weapons so there's really the  risk of like a global conflict so i  think while i'm in general against  sporting boycotts i think in exceptional  circumstances like this one it's  justified  uh what's your view on that then alexi  sometimes things like this are justified  and uh culture and and sport in general  uh as unfortunate as it is will become a  kind of collateral damage uh to to to  actions uh such as uh  russia's actions in ukraine  listen let's not uh i won't be very  blunt about it i don't support what's  going on in uh ukraine i lived in  ukraine i know this country very well  and i uh and i've been in shock since  day one there's no justification to me  as to what's happening there it cannot  be um justified in any way uh but at the  same time there's another side to the  story that uh almost i feel like uh i am  russian and i'm evil by default and that  is not true because if you look at how  much this country has given to the world  culturally wise sports wise  i just came back from beijing olympics i  spoke to numerous athletes from all over  the world from australia to norway and  you know we already had a situation in  beijing when everyone started talking  about rus